The assessment is written to session_plan/assessment.md (about 10 KB), but it isn't committed. session_plan/ is gitignored, so the commit command you gave fell through its || true. I didn't force-add it, so the planner reads it from disk.

State: the harness had the build and tests green at session start, and CI on HEAD 08435056 is green. My binary check worked: yoyo -p answered "OK" and exited 0. All of the last 10 sessions finished both tasks, with no reverts in 14 days. The only recurring CI failure was a hook test that died when a hook script never read its input (SIGPIPE). It was fixed at 10:19 and has been green since.

What I found:
• DeepSeek prices point opposite ways (#1084 row 2). yoagent prices DeepSeek at the peak rate on purpose, so its estimates come out high. yoyo's own price table (src/format/cost.rs) uses the off-peak rate, so /cost can come out low. What sets the peak rate is the UTC clock, and yoyo has the clock. So under the pricing rule written today this is something to fix now. There are two ways: copy yoagent and bill peak, or model the peak hours in a separate file, the way promo.rs already handles Google's sale dates by date. It has to be a separate file because cost.rs is at its size cap exactly (3640 / 3640 lines).
• Three wrong messages from shell commands are still there, each checked today:
  • yoyo watch true says "Watch mode ON" and exits 0, but the setting dies with the process (#1088).
  • Bare yoyo find and yoyo run print REPL usage like /find (#1086).
  • yoyo doctor names REPL-only commands (#1083).
• #1096 is a new outside request. It asks for weekly outreach on X with follower and star targets. The real question is who controls yoyo's public voice, so the right response is a reply naming @yuanhao, not code. .github/workflows/social.yml, which sets the schedule, is a protected file.
• Claude Code's two latest releases mostly match yoyo already. Their fix for overwriting a large file after reading only its start is the same remedy as partial_read_guard. Their "show output so far when a shell command times out" is already in yoagent's bash tool. One possible gap I haven't tested: their JSON output now escapes delete, C1 control and Unicode format characters. yoyo's JSON output goes through serde_json, which escapes only characters below 0x20, so it would likely print those raw. I saved the full comparison to yopedia.

